  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Living Herbarium",
    "overview": "FloraHearth is laid out like a well-kept herbarium: each crop, bed, jar and reading is a catalogued specimen with a clear name, a label and a place. Plant names are set in a literary serif, the way they would be written on a specimen sheet; labels and measurements are set in a small monospace, like the typed tags pinned beside them; everything else is a quiet, legible sans. The palette is drawn from the same cabinet: herbarium paper and sage washes by day, deep spruce and loam by night, with Hemlock Ink and New Shoot greens marking what can be acted on.\n\nThe mood is calm, precise and trustworthy. This is reference a gardener acts on (whether to cover tomatoes tonight, which canner a crop needs), so nothing shouts and nothing is decorative for its own sake. Density is high but ordered: small, consistent roles carry a lot of information, and amber and rose appear only when something genuinely needs attention. Depth is flat and tonal; hierarchy comes from surface steps, hairline borders and type, not from shadows.\n\nDark mode is the default and the night-garden face of the same herbarium; light mode is the paper face. Both must hold the same contrast and meaning.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Serif names, monospace labels and data, sans everything else",
      "Flat tonal surfaces with hairline borders; shadows only on dialogs",
      "Green means \"act here\"; amber means caution; rose means danger or loss",
      "Gently rounded, catalogue-card shapes (12–16px corners)",
      "Dark-first, with an equally careful light theme"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Green Means Go Rule",
        "body": "Hemlock Ink and New Shoot mark things the user can act on, or confirmed good news. Never use them as decoration or as a section background.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Status Colours Are Rare Rule",
        "body": "Amber and rose appear only when the content needs attention. If a screen is mostly amber, the warning has lost its meaning.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Faces Rule",
        "body": "Every colour pairing must pass WCAG AA in both themes, including hover and pressed states. White text never sits on a mid-tone (500–600) green, amber or rose; use the 700+ shade, or dark text on a light tint.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Specimen Label Rule",
        "body": "Monospace is for labels and measured data only. Plant and bed names are never monospace; they are serif.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 12px Floor Rule",
        "body": "No text below 0.75rem. Inputs render at 16px on touch screens so iOS never zooms on focus.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Real Weights Rule",
        "body": "Use only weights that are actually loaded (all three families load as variable fonts, 400–800). Italics come from Newsreader, the only family with an italic loaded: Latin names and quoted lore are Newsreader italic.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat-By-Default Rule",
        "body": "Hierarchy comes from tone, borders and type. A new shadow needs a reason: it must mean \"this is above the page\".",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use the theme tokens (`--surface-*`, `--text-*`, `--accent-*`) for new UI instead of new hex values; existing hard-coded hexes should move onto them over time.",
      "**Do** set plant and bed names in Newsreader, labels and measurements in JetBrains Mono, and everything else in Plus Jakarta Sans.",
      "**Do** check every new colour pairing at AA in both themes, including hover.",
      "**Do** keep text at 12px or larger and prose near 65 characters per line.",
      "**Do** make anything clickable a real button or link, with the item's name as its accessible name."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** put white text on 500–600 shades of green, amber or rose (they fail AA); use 700+ or dark text on a light tint.",
      "**Don't** add glass, backdrop blur or decorative shadows to navigation, cards or overlays.",
      "**Don't** introduce new near-identical greens; Hemlock Ink and New Shoot are the action colours.",
      "**Don't** use monospace for names or prose.",
      "**Don't** rely on a global dark-mode override for new components; give them explicit `dark:` values or tokens."
    ]
  }
}